1
00:00:00,801 --> 00:00:04,719
It's two a.m. Checkout is failing, and money is walking out the door.

2
00:00:04,719 --> 00:00:08,637
In the last few minutes, three changes went live. Which one broke it?

3
00:00:08,637 --> 00:00:17,015
Google's S.R.E. book says roughly seventy percent of outages come from changes. Roll back the wrong one, and you have just made it worse.

4
00:00:17,015 --> 00:00:23,084
That is the on-call engineer's question, night after night. Dashboards show symptoms, not causes.

5
00:00:23,084 --> 00:00:30,254
And today's A.I. agents answer with confidence, not proof. They guess, and some of them act on the guess.

6
00:00:30,362 --> 00:00:37,625
So we built Faultline. An on-call agent whose brain is a FalkorDB knowledge graph, and it lives by one rule.

7
00:00:37,625 --> 00:00:45,824
No path, no claim. It will not blame a change until the graph proves that the change is connected to the failure.

8
00:00:46,224 --> 00:00:53,757
Click the cube, and it opens into the real graph of this system: the services, the deploys, and the proof path between them.

9
00:00:53,757 --> 00:01:01,728
Here is where it sits. It reads what your stack already emits: alerts, deploys, the data warehouse, docs and decisions.

10
00:01:01,728 --> 00:01:11,237
One FalkorDB graph per tenant holds all of it. A crew of agents proposes; the evidence gate decides; and only then does anything act.

11
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:14,937
How it decides, step by step. The alert. The causal path.

12
00:01:14,937 --> 00:01:19,630
The flow that path can carry. The proof. The smallest cut that contains it.

13
00:01:19,630 --> 00:01:21,382
And one approved action.

14
00:01:21,382 --> 00:01:26,073
Every screen runs on that same graph: the incident room, the crew,

15
00:01:26,073 --> 00:01:32,288
the reasoning behind each verdict, the pre-deploy check, and the company brain.

16
00:01:32,447 --> 00:01:42,944
Let's run it. Checkout errors are spiking. The incident opens, and the crew gets to work: recall, scope, suspects, investigation, all recorded in the graph.

17
00:01:42,944 --> 00:01:47,740
Three suspects. The recommendation deploy has no causal path to checkout.

18
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:52,314
FalkorDB returns zero rows. So it cannot be blamed. Full stop.

19
00:01:52,314 --> 00:02:02,145
The email deploy does have a path. But max flow shows that path can carry only 2 percent of checkout's traffic, and we need far more to explain what we see.

20
00:02:02,145 --> 00:02:03,968
Rejected, with proof.

21
00:02:03,968 --> 00:02:11,760
Who did what? Every step is a record: which agent claimed it, which query it ran, and whom it handed to next.

22
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:16,359
The payment deploy passes every check. Here is the proof path, lit edge by edge.

23
00:02:16,359 --> 00:02:23,744
And here is why we believe it: every piece of evidence, traced back to the agent and the exact query that produced it.

24
00:02:24,029 --> 00:02:29,918
Max flow also finds the smallest cut that contains the damage, tried first on a copy of the graph.

25
00:02:29,918 --> 00:02:35,756
And the graph knows who should fix it: the people who wrote this code and fixed it last time.

26
00:02:35,756 --> 00:02:39,642
Now a human approves the exact change, bound to its plan hash.

27
00:02:39,642 --> 00:02:45,846
Faultline opens a real pull request on GitHub, merges it, and verifies it through a second channel.

28
00:02:45,846 --> 00:02:50,895
Applied. Written back into the graph, so the next incident starts warm.

29
00:03:04,028 --> 00:03:10,311
Is the graph just decoration? Cut one call in it, and the decision changes. The graph is load-bearing.

30
00:03:10,311 --> 00:03:18,443
The same graph works before the next deploy. This pull request drops payment retries, and it contradicts two decisions the team already made.

31
00:03:18,443 --> 00:03:20,135
Blocked, before it ships.

32
00:03:20,135 --> 00:03:25,740
And it is the team's memory. Ask who decided the payment timeout, and whether they are still here.

33
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:29,538
Every name in the answer comes from a query, drawn as a graph.

34
00:03:29,538 --> 00:03:37,864
Now, the numbers. Faultline gets every scenario right, where telemetry alone manages 62.5 percent,

35
00:03:37,864 --> 00:03:42,791
vector search 25 percent, and the latest deploy 0 percent.

36
00:03:42,791 --> 00:03:48,056
Across 140 adversarial worlds, zero confident wrong blames.

37
00:03:48,056 --> 00:03:51,354
And a strong language model on its own? 75 percent.

38
00:03:51,354 --> 00:03:54,652
It blames an innocent deploy when nothing is wrong.

39
00:03:54,652 --> 00:03:58,524
Put that same model inside Faultline's gate: 4 out of 4.

40
00:03:58,524 --> 00:04:03,655
Take FalkorDB's algorithms away and the correct answers fall from 100 to 56.

41
00:04:03,655 --> 00:04:09,651
Switch FalkorDB off, and it confirms nothing at all. That is what load-bearing means.

42
00:04:09,651 --> 00:04:16,887
And it is live right now: 5 out of 5 real runs, verified. Any agent can use the same read-only tools over M.C.P.

43
00:04:16,887 --> 00:04:22,980
Faultline. No path, no claim. Clone it, run make judge check, and verify it yourself.

